<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="guanine and guanosine salvage II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6599" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Many organisms can synthesize guanosine nucleotides &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt;. In that route |FRAME: GMP| (GMP) is synthesized via |FRAME: IMP| (IMP) and |FRAME: XANTHOSINE-5-PHOSPHATE| (XMP) , which is converted to GMP by the action of |FRAME: GMP-SYN-CPLX|, an enzyme that can use either glutamine or ammonia as substrate (see |FRAME: PWY-6125|). Note that the free base |FRAME: GUANINE| or the ribonucleoside |FRAME: GUANOSINE| are not produced via the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; pathway. Many organisms also use salvage enzymes to obtain purine bases and nucleosides and convert them to the corresponding nucleotides. The pathway described here is used by both prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms for this purpose. For example, all parasitic protozoa (e.g. |FRAME: TAX-5820|, |FRAME: TAX-5810|, |FRAME: TAX-38568|, |FRAME: TAX-5690|) lack the ability to synthesize purine nucleotides &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt;and use this salvage pathway to convert purine bases and nucleosides obtained from their hosts to the corresponding nucleotides |CITS: [3920982][6316234]|. After their uptake by specific transporters |CITS: [ 7443300][ 10743614]| the purine nucleosides are first hydrolyzed to ribose and a base by nucleoside hydrolases. The bases are then converted to the concomitant nucleotides by |FRAME: GPT-CPLX|, which utilizes |FRAME: PRPP| to convert the free base to the mononucleotide |FRAME: GMP|. Nucleoside hydrolases from parasites are attractive drug targets against parasitic protozoa since no such activity has been reported thus far in mammalian cells |CITS: [6396514]|. The pathway has also been described in green plants |CITS: [GURANOWSKI79]|. Other routes from |FRAME: GUANOSINE| to |FRAME: GMP| are described in |FRAME: PWY-6620| and |FRAME: PWY-6618|.</Description>
